# ReAct

## Overview

Your support Agent has to look up an order before it can answer
"Where is order A-1001?". `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct` runs that loop: a model
round asks for a tool, a tool round runs it, and the next model round writes
the answer. By the end of this Livebook, this request runs offline:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
reply = ask.("Where is order A-1001?", %{})

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls, reply.data.tool_rounds}
# => {:completed, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02.", 2, 1}
```

You'll read the ReAct graph, run model and tool rounds against a fake
provider, repair an empty answer, fall back to a second model, and stop the
loop with a limit. Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

`plug` is required by `Req.Test`, which stands in for the provider.

## Define the Agent

The tool is a normal Jido Action. Its name and Zoi schema become the model
tool definition:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the status and delivery estimate for one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), status: Zoi.string(), eta: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "support_agent", id: "my_app/support_agent", vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      instructions "You are a support agent. Look up orders before you answer."
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end
  end
end
```

The `reasoning` block sets defaults for the ReAct input schema. Each request
can override any of them except `input`.

## Read the Graph

ReAct is three Flow modules. Print their components:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct

for flow <- [MyApp.SupportAgent.reasoning(), ReAct.ModelRound, ReAct.ToolReview] do
  IO.puts("Graph: #{inspect(flow)}")

  flow.flow().components
  |> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 0))
  |> Enum.each(fn {name, component} -> IO.puts("  #{name}: #{component.kind}") end)
end
```

```text
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct
  finalize: call
  input_guard: call
  loop: invoke
  start_request: call
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.ModelRound
  detect_cycle: call
  model: call
  next: invoke
  output_guard: call
  project: call
  record_attempt: call
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.ToolReview
  decision: wait
  next: invoke
  request: call
```

| Flow | Component | Action | Job |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `ReAct` | `start_request` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.StartRequest` | Builds the turn: tools, limits, model list, and the user message in the Session |
| | `input_guard` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.GuardInput` | Rejects empty, oversized, or blocked input |
| | `loop` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.DecideInput` | Starts the first model round, or completes as `:blocked` |
| | `finalize` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.FinalizeTurn` | Records the outcome and returns `%{state:, response:}` |
| `ModelRound` | `project` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.ProjectConversation` | Builds a `Jido.AI.Context` from the Thread |
| | `model` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.CallModel` | Makes one ReqLLM call |
| | `output_guard` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.GuardOutput` | Checks the answer or the tool calls |
| | `record_attempt` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.RecordModelAttempt` | Saves the attempt and the accepted assistant message |
| | `detect_cycle` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.DetectToolCycle` | Flags a batch identical to the previous one |
| | `next` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.DecideNext` | Picks complete, model, tools, or review |

Each `invoke` component runs a decision Action, then
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExpandNext` turns that decision into the next
Flow: another `ModelRound`, a tool round, or `ToolReview`. The graph grows one
round at a time.

## Offline Model

This module plays the provider. It returns OpenAI Responses API JSON through
`Req.Test`, so ReqLLM runs its normal decoding path:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LLM do
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 256,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, __MODULE__}]
    }
  end

  def text(conn, text) do
    respond(conn, [
      %{
        "type" => "message",
        "id" => "msg_1",
        "role" => "assistant",
        "status" => "completed",
        "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
      }
    ])
  end

  def tool_call(conn, id, name, arguments) do
    respond(conn, [
      %{
        "type" => "function_call",
        "id" => "fc_" <> id,
        "call_id" => id,
        "name" => name,
        "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
        "status" => "completed"
      }
    ])
  end

  def rate_limited(conn) do
    conn
    |> Plug.Conn.put_status(429)
    |> Req.Test.json(%{"error" => %{"message" => "Rate limit reached", "type" => "rate_limit_error"}})
  end

  def body(conn) do
    {:ok, body, _conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
    Jason.decode!(body)
  end

  defp respond(conn, output) do
    Req.Test.json(conn, %{
      "id" => "resp_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 40,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 10,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 50
      }
    })
  end
end
```

`options` replaces the whole ReAct `options` default
(`%{max_tokens: 128, receive_timeout: 30_000, temperature: 0.0}`), so it sets
`max_tokens` and `temperature` again.

`ask` runs one request on a fresh Agent and returns the reply Signal:

```elixir
ask = fn input, extra ->
  agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
  params = Map.merge(%{input: input, options: MyApp.LLM.options()}, extra)
  signal = MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(params)

  {:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
    MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

  reply
end
```

## Run a Request

Queue two provider responses. `Req.Test.expect/2` serves them in order:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.tool_call(conn, "call_1", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"})
end)

Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.text(conn, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02.")
end)

reply = ask.("Where is order A-1001?", %{})

{reply.type, reply.data.status, reply.data.output}
```

The result is
`{"jido.ai.response", :completed, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02."}`.

```elixir
Map.take(reply.data, [:model_calls, :tool_rounds, :tool_calls, :tool_errors, :retries, :reason_code])
```

The result is
`%{model_calls: 2, tool_rounds: 1, tool_calls: 1, tool_errors: 0, retries: 0, reason_code: :none}`.

## Model Rounds

Each model round leaves one entry in `reply.data.model_attempts`:

```elixir
Enum.map(reply.data.model_attempts, fn attempt ->
  {attempt.model_call, attempt.outcome.kind, attempt.outcome.finish_reason, attempt.output_allowed}
end)
```

The result is
`[{1, :tool_calls, :tool_calls, true}, {2, :final_answer, :stop, true}]`.

After the output guard, `DecideNext` routes the round. The first matching row
wins:

| Model outcome | Next |
| --- | --- |
| Budget refused the call | Complete `:blocked`, `reason_code: :limits` |
| Provider error | Retry, then fall back, else complete `:failed`, `reason_code: :model` |
| Output guard rejected it | Repair round, then fall back, else complete `:blocked`, `reason_code: :output` |
| Tool calls, `tool_rounds` reached | Complete `:blocked`, `"maximum tool rounds reached"` |
| Tool calls, `tool_calls` would be exceeded | Complete `:blocked`, `"maximum tool calls reached"` |
| Tool calls that need review | `ToolReview` |
| Tool calls | Tool round |
| Final answer | Complete `:completed` |

The output guard rejects a tool-call response with more than 4 calls,
duplicate call IDs, a tool outside the request's tool selection, or arguments
that fail the Action schema. It rejects a final answer that is empty, longer
than 4,000 characters, missing required structured data, stopped by
`:length` or `:content_filter`, or sent before a required tool round.

## Tool Rounds

A tool round is a Flow built at runtime from the model's calls. Build one for
two lookups to see its shape:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExpandNext

{:ok, selection} = Jido.AI.Toolset.select([MyApp.LookupOrder])

calls = [
  %{id: "call_1", name: "lookup_order", arguments: %{"order_id" => "A-1001"}},
  %{id: "call_2", name: "lookup_order", arguments: %{"order_id" => "A-1002"}}
]

input = %{turn: %{tools: selection.tools, model_outcome: %{tool_calls: calls}}}
{:continue, ^input, tool_flow} = ExpandNext.run(%{kind: :tools, input: input}, %{})

IO.puts("Graph: runtime Parallel tool calls")

tool_flow.components
|> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 0))
|> Enum.each(fn
  {name, %{kind: :parallel, actions: actions}} ->
    IO.puts("  #{name}: parallel")
    Enum.each(actions, &IO.puts("    #{&1.name}: #{inspect(&1.call.target)}"))

  {name, component} ->
    IO.puts("  #{name}: #{component.kind}")
end)
```

```text
Graph: runtime Parallel tool calls
  model: call
  record: call
  tools: parallel
    tool_0: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExecuteTool
    tool_1: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExecuteTool
```

`tools` runs every call at once and collects errors instead of stopping.
`record` (`Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.RecordToolResults`) appends the
results to the Session in call order and adds one to `tool_rounds`. `model`
starts the next `ModelRound`. A failed tool becomes an error result for the
model; it doesn't fail the request.

The first request's tool round left one attempt:

```elixir
[attempt] = reply.data.tool_attempts
Map.take(attempt, [:tool_call_id, :tool_name, :status, :input, :output, :tool_round])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  tool_call_id: "call_1",
  tool_name: "lookup_order",
  status: :ok,
  input: %{"order_id" => "A-1001"},
  output: %{"eta" => "2026-11-02", "order_id" => "A-1001", "status" => "shipped"},
  tool_round: 1
}
```

The model saw `output`, cut to `limits.tool_output_bytes` (default 16,384).
[Tool Execution and Results](tool-execution-and-results.md) covers that
contract.

## Repair Bad Output

An empty answer fails the output guard. With the default `limits.retries` of
1, ReAct adds a repair instruction and calls the model again:

```elixir
notebook = self()

Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn -> MyApp.LLM.text(conn, "   ") end)

Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  send(notebook, {:last_message, List.last(MyApp.LLM.body(conn)["input"])})
  MyApp.LLM.text(conn, "Refunds post within 5 business days.")
end)

reply = ask.("How long do refunds take?", %{})

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls, reply.data.retries}
```

The result is `{:completed, "Refunds post within 5 business days.", 2, 1}`.
The fake provider doesn't run in the notebook process, so it reports to the
pid captured as `notebook`. The repair request ends with a system message:

```elixir
receive do
  {:last_message, %{"role" => role, "content" => [%{"text" => text}]}} -> {role, text}
after
  1_000 -> :no_request
end
```

The result is
`{"system", "Repair the previous response. model output is empty. Return a valid final answer."}`.
The rejected answer is in `model_attempts` with `output_allowed: false` but
never enters the conversation.

## Fallback Models

A retryable provider error (timeout, 429, 5xx, or a closed connection) first
retries the same model while `limits.retries` allows it, then moves to the
next entry in `fallback_models`. Set `retries: 0` to fall back on the first
error:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, &MyApp.LLM.rate_limited/1)

Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.text(conn, "Refunds post within 5 business days.")
end)

reply =
  ask.("How long do refunds take?", %{
    fallback_models: ["openai:gpt-4.1-mini"],
    limits: %{retries: 0}
  })

{reply.data.status, reply.data.model_calls, reply.data.retries,
 Enum.map(reply.data.model_attempts, &{&1.model, &1.status, &1.outcome[:category]})}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:completed, 2, 1,
 [{"openai:gpt-4o-mini", :error, :rate_limit}, {"openai:gpt-4.1-mini", :ok, nil}]}
```

A fallback costs a model call and a retry, and each switch resets the attempt
count. A non-retryable error, such as a 401, completes as `:failed` with
`reason_code: :model` without trying the fallbacks. The `max_retries: 0` in
`MyApp.LLM.options/0` turns off ReqLLM's own HTTP retries so that ReAct owns
the retry decision.

## Limits Stop the Loop

`limits.tool_rounds: 0` lets the model ask for a tool but never run one:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.tool_call(conn, "call_2", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1002"})
end)

reply = ask.("Where is order A-1002?", %{limits: %{tool_rounds: 0}})

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :reason_code, :reason, :output, :model_calls, :tool_rounds])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  status: :blocked,
  reason_code: :limits,
  reason: "maximum tool rounds reached",
  output: "",
  model_calls: 1,
  tool_rounds: 0
}
```

| Limit | Default | Range |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `model_calls` | 8 | 1–16 |
| `tool_rounds` | 4 | 0–8 |
| `tool_calls` | 8 | 1–16 |
| `retries` | 1 | 0–4 |
| `elapsed_ms` | 120,000 | 1–3,600,000 |
| `tool_output_bytes` | 16,384 | 256–1,000,000 |
| `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `total_tokens`, `total_cost` | `nil` (off) | ≥ 1, cost ≥ 0 |

[Budgets, Retries, and Fallbacks](budgets-retries-and-fallbacks.md) explains
how each budget is counted.

## Final Result

Every path ends in `FinalizeTurn` with one terminal result:

| `status` | `reason_code` | `output` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:completed` | `:none` | The structured object when `output_schema` is set, else the answer text |
| `:blocked` | `:input`, `:review`, `:output`, or `:limits` | `""` |
| `:failed` | `:model` | `""` |

`reason` carries the guard or limit message. `reasoning` and
`reasoning_steps` are `nil` for ReAct; the single-call and multi-stage Flows
fill them. The same summary is recorded in the Session Thread as an
`:ai_request_outcome` entry.

## Summary

- `ReAct` starts a request, guards input, invokes a loop of `ModelRound`
  Flows, and finalizes one result.
- `DecideNext` routes each model outcome to completion, a repair or retry
  round, a fallback model, a tool round, or tool review.
- A tool round runs all calls in parallel, records results in call order, and
  starts the next model round.
- Rejected output gets a repair instruction while `limits.retries` allows it.
- Retryable provider errors retry, then move through `fallback_models`.
- Limits end the loop as `:blocked` with `reason_code: :limits`.

